  "strategy": "Apprellance Detection Based Optimization\nRationale:\nThe provided code uses a sophisticated multi-phase optimization strategy, but it tends to get stuck in local optima. This approach introduces two key novelties:\n1. Started with a three-point potential template (simplified cost function) that ensures the function is not constant but has concavity patterns similar to the original initial solution.\n2. Employed a rounding scheme that favors integer-valued h values (automatically passing boundary conditions) while allowing a short optimization period to ensure competitiveness.\n\nMathematical Specification:\nVariables:\n- n_points: grid size (300, 100, or 50 if rounding required)\n- h: step function with values in [0,1] such that \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\nObjective:\n- Minimize C5 = max_k \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\nConstraints:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- \u2211 h[i] \u2248 n_points/2 (with careful rounding)\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   - Start from initial_h_values (n=300)\n   - For other grid sizes, generate templates from parent: h_parent\n2. Modification:\n   three_point_oscillator_template:\n   - Apply template modifications that resemble early stages of convolution-based optimization\n   - Preserve boundary conditions and ensure integral=1\n3. Iterative Process:\n   - If n=300: use binary_push() for 200 steps then Adam_optimize()\n   - If n=100: use the three-point template and set h[i] to integers {0,1,2} then clip to [0,1]\n   - If n=50: approximate parent through interpolation and binary_push\n4. Validation:\n   - Ensure \u222bh=1 by rounding-adjustment (simple clipping/adjustment)\n   - Maintain feasible set throughout optimization\n   - Return best candidate from {300,100,50} or use parent if rounding causes discontinuity\n\nSearch Choices:\n- Base n=300, scheduled reductions to 100 and 50\n- Template modifications controlled by two oscillators\n- Rounding strategy: prefer integer values near 0/1 boundaries\n\nComputational Budget:\n\u23f1\ufe0f Primary times: 400s for 300 search, 200s for 100 interpolation, 100s for 50 fallback\n\u23f1\ufe0f Checkpoint: 600s for 300 parent optimization\n\u23f1\ufe0f Early termination at round(trial,10): 20s per ps3 trial\n\nValidation & Return Contract:\n- Compute C5 for both parent and optimized candidates\n- Must review feasibility and IE values\n- Return function candidate if improved, else report dead end",
